from V8s to 14-cylinder twin-row rotary piston engines\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e24 B-24s built in all of 1942; 1,000 within a little over a year; 2,000 six months after that\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBy 1944: one plane leaving every 55 minutes\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eFactory closed June 28, 1945; total production: 8,685 B-24s\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e6,792 flew out under their own power\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e1,893 built as knockdown kits, hauled on 70+ ft trailers (with tandem V8 tractors) to Douglas in Tulsa, OK and North American in Grand Prairie, TX\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eThe famous right turn in the assembly line: Henry Ford forced it to route deliveries into Washtenaw County and avoid Wayne County taxes\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eEast wall of the factory sat just six feet west of the county line\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003eThe B-24 Itself: Design, Crew, and Variants\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWingspan: 110 ft; ~1.25 million parts, including ~350,000 rivets\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRiveting was a two-person job; many “Rosies” were 17–18-year-old women straight out of high school\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRivet gun weighed enough that eight hours a day built the famous biceps\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWaist gunners stood in open windows at 28,000 ft, 275–285 mph, in temperatures as low as -75°F in winter\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003ePlexiglas not added until late 1943; plane was never pressurized\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eCrew layered wool underwear, electric flight suits (plug-in), 45 lb flak vest, and flak helmet\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eE model: no nose guns; Luftwaffe quickly learned to attack head-on\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eJ model: added nose turret; bare metal finish (paint added 120 lbs, cost 8–20 mph)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eNavy PB4Y-2 variant: single tail, normally aspirated engines, no belly turret, bubble waist windows for sub spotting\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eProduction bays built 150 ft wide (vs. 110 ft B-24 wingspan) on General Hap Arnold’s insistence, to accommodate the B-29 (143 ft wingspan) if needed\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003ePost-War History and Notable Stories\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eAfter the war, Ford declined the plant; it sat empty for two years\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eUniversity of Michigan bought the airport for $1 (with radar research obligation); ground-penetrating radar developed here\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eKaiser-Frazer leased it 1947–1953: built the Henry J, rear-tine rototillers, and 73 C-119 Flying Boxcars\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eGM bought it in August 1953 after their Livonia Hydramatic plant burned down; first transmission off the line November 1953\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003ePlant grew to 5 million sq ft under GM; closed 2010 due to GM bankruptcy\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eTorn down; became the American Center for Mobility; museum saved the last ~3% (144,000 sq ft rollout hangar)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eDoolittle Raid (April 1942): 16 B-25s launched from a carrier ~600 miles out (early launch after being spotted); bombed six Japanese cities\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eDoolittle expected court-martial for losing 16 aircraft and 5 men; received Medal of Honor and promotion to Brigadier General\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eStrategic damage minimal, but Japanese morale collapsed and crack fighter squadrons were pulled from front lines\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eMarie Mountain Clark, local WASP pilot: member of the Caterpillar Club (unintentional bailout)\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBaggy men’s flight suit snagged her seatbelt latch during spin training; ejected during a weightlessness maneuver over Sweetwater, TX\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eLanded in a cotton field, hiked to the road, and hitchhiked back\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eHer husband John Alden Clark: B-17 co-pilot, later Dean of Engineering at University of Michigan; died two years ago at age 100\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"12:[\"$\",\"$L23\",null,{\"resourceId\":\"345ca9bf-0f87-4712-8db5-dce8876b1272\",\"resourceType\":\"document\",\"creatorName\":\"Chris Sorensen\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L24\",null,{\"joinName\":\"Chris Sorensen\",\"type\":\"meeting\",\"variant\":\"modal\",\"creatorInfo\":{\"name\":\"Chris Sorensen\",\"avatar\":\"https://d2y2qtsuzmwn83.cloudfront.net/f9164abfa6b7baae860a51927564c7d6?v=3\",\"workspaceName\":null}}],[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"className\":\"relative flex w-screen flex-col overflow-hidden bg-white supports-[height:100cqh]:h-[100cqh] supports-[height:100svh]:h-[100svh]\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L25\",null,{\"documentId\":\"345ca9bf-0f87-4712-8db5-dce8876b1272\",\"documentPanel\":{\"document\":{\"id\":\"345ca9bf-0f87-4712-8db5-dce8876b1272\",\"title\":\"Michigan Flight Museum\",\"created_at\":\"2026-07-23T16:39:35.124Z\",\"ydoc_version\":2,\"owner\":{\"id\":\"870a61ad-2e3b-4cb7-b9ce-fe467e7e57f7\",\"name\":\"Chris Sorensen\",\"picture\":\"https://d2y2qtsuzmwn83.cloudfront.net/f9164abfa6b7baae860a51927564c7d6?v=3\"}},\"panel\":{\"id\":\"3e572d7c-cb09-4934-a31f-d3d54271cd62\",\"document_id\":\"345ca9bf-0f87-4712-8db5-dce8876b1272\",\"title\":\"Summary\",\"content\":{\"type\":\"doc\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"heading\",\"attrs\":{\"id\":null,\"level\":3},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Willow Run Bomber Plant: Origins and Construction\"}]},{\"type\":\"bulletList\",\"attrs\":{\"tight\":true},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"listItem\",\"attrs\":{\"id\":null},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"attrs\":{\"id\":null},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\